    <item>
      <title>How does this tool efficiently calculate error budget over long SLO windows?</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/blog/riemann-sum-error-budget-calculation/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 16 Jan 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/blog/riemann-sum-error-budget-calculation/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;This tool uses a Riemann Sum-inspired technique to efficiently calculate error budget consumption over long time windows (like 30 days) without requiring expensive range queries over raw counter metrics.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-problem&#34;&gt;The Problem&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Calculating error budget remaining over a 30-day window naively would require something like:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;div style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;table style=&#34;border-spacing:0;padding:0;margin:0;border:0;&#34;&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style=&#34;vertical-align:top;padding:0;margin:0;border:0;&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;&#34;&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;white-space:pre;-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none;margin-right:0.4em;padding:0 0.4em 0 0.4em;color:#7f7f7f&#34;&gt;1&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;&lt;td style=&#34;vertical-align:top;padding:0;margin:0;border:0;;width:100%&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-promql&#34; data-lang=&#34;promql&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;sum_over_time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;rate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;errors[&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;5m&lt;/span&gt;]&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;[&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;30d&lt;/span&gt;]&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;sum_over_time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;rate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;total[&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;5m&lt;/span&gt;]&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;[&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;30d&lt;/span&gt;]&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is computationally expensive and can cause Prometheus query timeouts, especially with high cardinality metrics.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-riemann-sum-solution&#34;&gt;The Riemann Sum Solution&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Instead, this tool generates a recording rule that pre-computes the error ratio at 5-minute intervals:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Understanding SLO-Based Alerting</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/blog/understanding-slo-based-alerting/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 15 Jan 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/blog/understanding-slo-based-alerting/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Service Level Objective (SLO) based alerting is a powerful approach to monitoring that focuses on user-impacting reliability issues rather than arbitrary thresholds.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;what-is-an-slo&#34;&gt;What is an SLO?&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;An SLO is a target reliability level for a service, expressed as a percentage. For example, a 99.9% availability SLO means your service should be available 99.9% of the time over a given period.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;why-slo-based-alerting&#34;&gt;Why SLO-Based Alerting?&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Traditional alerting often uses static thresholds like &amp;ldquo;alert if error rate &amp;gt; 5%&amp;rdquo;. This approach has problems:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>How do I query these generated rules in Prometheus to monitor my application?</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/blog/querying-generated-prometheus-rules/</link>
      <pubDate>Tue, 14 Jan 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/blog/querying-generated-prometheus-rules/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Once you&amp;rsquo;ve loaded the generated rules into Prometheus, you can query the recording rules directly to monitor your application&amp;rsquo;s health and SLO compliance. Here are the most useful queries:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;check-current-slo-status&#34;&gt;Check Current SLO Status&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;See if your service is currently meeting its SLO target:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;div style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;table style=&#34;border-spacing:0;padding:0;margin:0;border:0;&#34;&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style=&#34;vertical-align:top;padding:0;margin:0;border:0;&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;&#34;&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;white-space:pre;-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none;margin-right:0.4em;padding:0 0.4em 0 0.4em;color:#7f7f7f&#34;&gt;1&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;white-space:pre;-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none;margin-right:0.4em;padding:0 0.4em 0 0.4em;color:#7f7f7f&#34;&gt;2&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;white-space:pre;-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none;margin-right:0.4em;padding:0 0.4em 0 0.4em;color:#7f7f7f&#34;&gt;3&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;white-space:pre;-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none;margin-right:0.4em;padding:0 0.4em 0 0.4em;color:#7f7f7f&#34;&gt;4&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;white-space:pre;-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none;margin-right:0.4em;padding:0 0.4em 0 0.4em;color:#7f7f7f&#34;&gt;5&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&#xA;&lt;td style=&#34;vertical-align:top;padding:0;margin:0;border:0;;width:100%&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-promql&#34; data-lang=&#34;promql&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;# Success rate over the last 5 minutes&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;job&lt;span style=&#34;color:#960050;background-color:#1e0010&#34;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;slo&lt;span style=&#34;color:#960050;background-color:#1e0010&#34;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;ratio_rate5m{job&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&amp;#34;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;my-app&lt;/span&gt;&amp;#34;}&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;# Compare to your SLO goal&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;job&lt;span style=&#34;color:#960050;background-color:#1e0010&#34;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;slo&lt;span style=&#34;color:#960050;background-color:#1e0010&#34;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;ratio_rate5m{job&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&amp;#34;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;my-app&lt;/span&gt;&amp;#34;} &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; job&lt;span style=&#34;color:#960050;background-color:#1e0010&#34;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;slo_goal&lt;span style=&#34;color:#960050;background-color:#1e0010&#34;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;ratio{job&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&amp;#34;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;my-app&lt;/span&gt;&amp;#34;}&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;A value of &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt; (true) means you&amp;rsquo;re meeting your SLO, &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt; (false) means you&amp;rsquo;re currently below target.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>How do I size my Prometheus deployment?</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/blog/sizing-prometheus-deployment/</link>
      <pubDate>Sun, 12 Jan 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/blog/sizing-prometheus-deployment/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Properly sizing a Prometheus deployment is critical for reliable monitoring. Our Resource Calculator helps you estimate memory, CPU, and disk requirements based on your metrics workload.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;the-three-key-inputs&#34;&gt;The Three Key Inputs&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Active Time Series&lt;/strong&gt;: The number of unique time series your Prometheus instance tracks&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Scrape Interval&lt;/strong&gt;: How often Prometheus collects metrics (default: 60 seconds)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Retention Period&lt;/strong&gt;: How long to store historical data (default: 30 days)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;finding-your-active-time-series-count&#34;&gt;Finding Your Active Time Series Count&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;If you already have Prometheus running, query:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>How to Calculate Prometheus Resource Requirements</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/blog/how-to-calculate-prometheus-resources/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 08 Jan 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/blog/how-to-calculate-prometheus-resources/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Sizing your Prometheus deployment correctly is crucial for reliable monitoring. Here&amp;rsquo;s how to calculate the resources you need.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;key-metrics&#34;&gt;Key Metrics&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Three factors determine Prometheus resource requirements:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Active time series&lt;/strong&gt;: Number of unique metric streams&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Scrape interval&lt;/strong&gt;: How often Prometheus collects metrics (default: 60s)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Retention period&lt;/strong&gt;: How long to keep historical data (default: 15 days)&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;memory-requirements&#34;&gt;Memory Requirements&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Prometheus keeps recent data in memory for fast queries.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rule of thumb&lt;/strong&gt;: 7-7.5 KiB per active time series&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
